111:
The three-origin (ti-samuṭṭhāna) category arises through either the first three origins or the last three origins, and not by other origins. The four-origin (catu-samuṭṭhāna) category arises through the first, third, fourth, and sixth origins, or through the second, third, fifth, and sixth origins, and not by other origins. The six-origin (cha-samuṭṭhāna) category arises through all six origins.

Thus:
The single-origin (eka-samuṭṭhāna) has 3 variations;
The two-origin (dvi-samuṭṭhāna) has 5 variations;
The three-origin and four-origin each have 2 variations;
The six-origin has only 1 variation.

By way of origin, all offenses are of 13 types. These offenses acquire 13 names according to the origin of the first-enacted training rule. They are: the origin of the first Pārājika, the origin of Adinnādāna, the origin of Sañcaritta, the origin of Samanubhāsana, the origin of Kathina, the origin of Eḷakaloma, the origin of Padasodhamma, the origin of Addhāna, the origin of Theyyasattha, the origin of Dhammadesanā, the origin of Bhūtārocana, the origin of Corivuṭṭhāpana, and the origin of Ananuññāta. Among these 13, the offense that arises due to body and mind is called the origin of the first Pārājika. The offense that arises through the three origins accompanied by consciousness (sacittaka) is called the origin of Adinnādāna. The offense that arises through all six origins is called the origin of Sañcaritta. The offense that arises only through the sixth origin is called the origin of Samanubhāsana. The offense that arises through the third and sixth origins is called the origin of Kathina. The offense that arises through the first and fourth origins is called the origin of Eḷakaloma. The offense that arises through the second and fifth origins is called the origin of Padasodhamma. The offense that arises through the first, third, fourth, and sixth origins is called the origin of Addhāna. The offense that arises through the fourth and sixth origins is called the origin of Theyyasattha. The offense that arises only through the fifth origin is called the origin of Dhammadesanā. The offense that arises through the three origins without consciousness (acittaka) is called the origin of Bhūtārocana. The offense that arises through the fifth and sixth origins is called the origin of Corivuṭṭhāpana. The offense that arises through the second, third, fifth, and sixth origins is called the origin of Ananuññāta. Thus, the decision in all cases should be understood by way of this classification of origins.

"Difference by way of action, perception, and consciousness" (kiri-yā-saññā-cittena nānattaṃ) means that by knowing the distinct variations of all offenses through these actions, etc., the decision in all cases should be understood. All offenses are of five types by way of action (kiriya). They are: an offense arising from committing a prohibited act (kiriya); an offense arising from not performing a required duty (akiriya); an offense arising from both action and inaction (kiriya-akiriya); an offense arising sometimes from action and sometimes from inaction; and an offense arising sometimes from action and sometimes from both action and inaction.

Among these five, an offense that occurs in the continuum of a monk who commits a prohibited act with the body or speech, such as digging the earth, is said to arise from action (kiriya). An offense that occurs in the continuum of a monk who does not perform a required duty with the body or speech, like the first Kathina offense, is said to arise from inaction (akiriya). An offense that occurs from both committing what should not be done and not doing what should be done, like the offense of receiving a robe from the hand of an unrelated nun, is said to arise from action-inaction (kiriya-akiriya). An offense that arises sometimes from action and sometimes from inaction, like the offense of receiving gold and silver, is said to arise sometimes from action and sometimes from inaction. An offense that arises sometimes from action and sometimes from both action and inaction, like the Kuṭikāra offense, is said to arise sometimes from action and sometimes from both.

Furthermore, all offenses are of two types by way of perception: offenses released by perception (saññā-vimokkha) and offenses not released by perception (no-saññā-vimokkha).
